Article 10

(1) The Regional Government is obliged and responsible for providing Protection to Women and Children Victims of Violence.

2. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children victims of violence established by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
c. collecting data and information about women and children victims of violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;
e. establishing PPT;
10-
f. allocating budget in the implementation of Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence; and
g. fostering, supervising and evaluating the implementation of Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence.

Article 16

SPM as referred to in Article 15 has performance indicators including:
a. coverage of Women and Children Victims of Violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers in integrated service units;
b. coverage of Women and Children Victims of Violence who receive health services by trained health personnel at Puskesmas and Hospitals;
c. coverage of Social Rehabilitation services provided by trained Social Rehabilitation officers for Women and Children Victims of Violence in integrated service units;
d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for Women and Children Victims of Violence in integrated service units;
13-
e. coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level to court decisions on cases of Violence against Women and Children;
f. coverage of Women and Children Victims of Violence who receive legal assistance services;
g. coverage of Repatriation services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
h. coverage of social reintegration services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
1. coverage of legal assistance services from the Investigation level to Court decisions for children in conflict with the law.

Article 15

SPM for Integrated Services for Women and Children, includes services:  a. handling complaints/reports of Victims of Violence against Women and Children;
b. health services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
c. law enforcement and legal assistance for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
d. repatriation for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal for Women and Children Victims of Violence; and f. psychological services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;

Article 11

1. The obligations and responsibilities of society as referred to in Article 9 letter b, are implemented in the form of community participation.
(2) Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), include:
a. preventing acts of Violence against victims;
b. establishing complaint posts at the sub-district/village level;
c. establishing Women and Children Protection units within community organizations;
d. conducting independent socialization of Women's rights and Children's rights;
e. providing first aid to victims; and
f. reporting to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, customary institutions, the private sector, and mass media.
4. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), are implemented responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

REGENT OF NORTH BUTON,

Considering Whereas
a. that one of the State's objectives to be achieved is to protect the entire Indonesian nation and all Indonesian territory so that every citizen has the right to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and has the right to obtain security and freedom from all forms of violence;
b. that violence against women and children in North Buton Regency shows a fairly high number and continues to increase, so protection efforts are needed;
c. that to provide a foundation and legal certainty for the protection of women and children victims of violence, it needs to be stipulated by Regional Regulation;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to stipulate a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection [State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia - -- ~-------------------------------- -2- Year 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
3. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
4. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635) as amended by Law Number 13 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4602);
5. Law Number J 4 of 2007 concerning Establishment of North Buton Regency in Southeast Sulawesi Province [State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 16, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4690);
6. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
7. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 11 of 2020 concerning Job Creation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2020 Number 245, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6573);
8. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims -3- of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
9. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Implementation of Women's Protection;
10. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Implementation of Child Protection;
11. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 80 of 2015 concerning Formation of Regional Legal Products (State News of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 2036) as amended by Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 120 of 2018 concerning Amendment to Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 80 of 2015 concerning Formation of Regional Legal Products (State News of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2018 Number 157);

With Mutual Approval REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF NORTH BUTON REGENCY and

CHAPTER VI

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 23 ( 1) The Regional Government conducts guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection for Women and Children victims of violence.

2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:  a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. provision of facilities;
d. monitoring; and e. evaluation.
3. Supervision as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be carried out with the principles of: a. professional;
b. transparent; and c. accountable.

Article 24 Guidance and supervision as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (2) and paragraph (3), shall be carried out in order to realize the objectives of implementing protection for Women and Children victims of violence in accordance with SPM established by the provisions of laws and regulations.
16-

Article 14

1. In providing Protection to Women and Children Victims of Violence, PPT is obliged to: a. provide services as quickly as possible and free of charge to Victims;
b. organize Protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to Medical Rehabilitation, Social Rehabilitation, Repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance;
c. cooperate with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants needed for Victims;
d. network with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of Victims and cooperate with witness and Victim protection institutions, social Protection homes or government, community, or other institution-owned trauma centers for Victim health recovery;
e. provide convenience, comfort, and safety for Victims;
f. maintain confidentiality of Victims;
g. provide economic empowerment;
12-
h. provide fulfillment of legal assistance for Victims; and  1. in the event that integrated service delivery is carried out in a networked manner, PPT remains responsible for the entire referral process of services required by Victims.
2. The provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with SPM.

Article 19

(I) Forms of services provided to women and children victims of violence, are as follows:
a. complaint services;
14-
b. health services;
c. legal assistance;
d. repatriation;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. psychological services and;
g. economic empowerment.
2. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:  a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and d. mental and spiritual.
3. In carrying out duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the Regional Government cooperates with government agencies, provincial government, other district/city governments, communities, families and parents.
4. Further provisions regarding procedures for services, and handling of women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph {l), paragraph {2), and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER II

FORMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 2

Forms of violence against Women and Children are:
a. Physical Violence;
b. Psychological Violence;
c. Sexual Violence;
d. Neglect; ande. Exploitation.

Article 5

Sexual Violence as referred to in Article 2 letter c is caused by:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/ord. forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.

Article 6

Neglect as referred to in Article 2 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of the child properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after the child as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts that neglect a person within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependence by means of restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS AND CHILDREN IN CONFLICT WITH THE LAW

Article 8

1. Every victim has the right to obtain:
a. protection from individuals, groups and institutions both governmental and non-governmental;
b. access and information about the existence of complaint centers, PPT, and all matters related to the fulfillment of rights and involvement in the decision-making process related to assistance and case development;
c. services that include medical, extensive medicolegal, psychosocial and legal;
d. handling that does not stop at physical and psychological healing, but until the victim can resume life in society including in the restoration of their good name;
e. confidential handling which includes guarantees of certainty for the victim that their identity will not be disseminated, medical care and legal handling;
f. assistance provided by psychologists to victims suffering from trauma/other mental problems to restore the victim's mental condition;
9-
g. legal aid assistance provided by persons and/or legal aid institutions to victims at every level of examination and throughout the legal process; and

h. guarantees of rights related to the victim's status.

2. Every child in conflict with the law has the right to obtain:
a. the right to confidentiality and non-disclosure of their identity;
b. the right to obtain Legal Aid at every stage of examination, from investigation to the sentencing stage; and
c. the right to obtain assistance at every stage of examination, from investigation to the sentencing stage.

